g came directly from Latin via the Vulgate Bible, where it was used to translate the Hebrew _goyim_ — the nations other than Israel. This gives us Y, used in English to mean “a non-Jew.” The third borrowing was a 16th-century re-borrowing of the same French word, this time spelled to retain the upper-class flavor that the older word X had lost. This gives us Z, an adjective for an aspirationally aristocratic style that has a different vowel sound in its second syllable than Y. ID X (5 points), Y (5 points), and Z (5 points).", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:42:16Z", "username": "Nikhil Borra", "text": "Gentile", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:42:22Z", "username": "Nikhil Borra", "text": "Gentle", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:42:22Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Genteel", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:42:23Z", "username": "Yash Agrawal", "text": "gentle", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:42:36Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "Answers: X=gentle, Y=gentile, Z=genteel, all from the Latin word gentilis", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:42:59Z", "username": "Abhishek Saluja", "text": "Just got here, achha hua maine genital nahi guess kiya", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:42:59Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "Nilesh: 20, Yash: 25, Akshay: 5, Nikhil: 20, Srijan: 25", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:43:25Z", "username": "Yash Agrawal", "text": "sabke mann ki baat boldi.", "role": "chat", "is_